e.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Pete Ricketts (R) is running.", "Dan Osborn is an independent standing in for the Democratic side."]}, "overview": "Nebraska's Senate race pits appointed incumbent Pete Ricketts against independent Dan Osborn, who is backed by Democrats but running outside the party. The state's Republican lean gives Ricketts a structural advantage, and he has out-raised Osborn by roughly two to one while a GOP super PAC has added $1.4 million in advertising. Osborn, by contrast, has drawn little outside help.\n\nPolling has been unusually close, with recent surveys split between a four-point Ricketts lead and a four-point Osborn lead, though several polls came from Democratic-leaning sponsors. Markets still price Ricketts near 69 percent. The leaked recording of Osborn criticizing Trump voters has become the race's central controversy, and most forecasters rate the race Likely R, with a few at Lean R.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1905, "margin": -4.44}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1701, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.215, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2151, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1705, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.217, "margin": -4.0}]}